From dad767d075abc47e820a73b6c66e1ffcaa2f0176 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Heinz Wiesinger <heinz.accounts+anthropic@wiesinger.pro>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 21:06:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] splash: Only scale the visible part of images

Splash::drawImage() used to scale every image to its full size on the
device and colour convert all of it before blitting, even when only a
small part of it is visible. When rendering a tile of a zoomed in page,
or an image that is clipped to a small region, nearly all of that work
is thrown away, and it grows with the zoom level: a 1920x1080 tile of a
page with a full page 3508x2480 image at 800 dpi means scaling the image
to 9355x6613 pixels.

Now drawImage() computes the part of the scaled image that can be
visible given the clip (including the bounding box of clip paths) and
the bitmap, and the scaling functions only produce that part. Source
rows above the visible part are skipped without being converted (image
sources now accept a nullptr colorLine to skip a line), and source rows
below it are not read at all.

The output is identical to before: the scaling functions use the same
arithmetic and just skip storing the pixels outside the crop rectangle.

Rendering a single page with 11 full-page JPEG images, 10 of them clipped
to small regions, with the Qt6 frontend:

                         before     after
  full page, 10 dpi       834 ms    353 ms
  full page, 96 dpi      1372 ms    479 ms
  full page, 200 dpi     2673 ms    772 ms
  1920x1080 at 400 dpi  10515 ms    512 ms
  1920x1080 at 800 dpi  33317 ms    528 ms

